INT. RADIO DUGOUT, WESTERN FRONT - NIGHT

A timber box dug into clay. A single candle. A field set with a cracked dial.

LIEUTENANT PHILIPPE ARMAND-DOGBE (20s, mud to the eyebrows, a hole in his left sleeve) crouches over the headset, writing coordinates with a stub of pencil.

Static. Then a faint, courteous voice.

RADIO (V.O.)
(distant, crackling)
Dugout Seven, this is Battalion. Report.

PHILIPPE
(into the mic)
Battalion, Dugout Seven. Quiet night. Nothing to report.

RADIO (V.O.)
Copy. Stay warm, Seven.

PHILIPPE
Will do.

He releases the key. Stares at the candle. The flame is steady. Too steady. For a long moment the whole dugout seems to hold still.

A scrape at the entrance. The blanket curtain moves.

A figure ducks in. A boy in a muddy overcoat with no insignia, holding a tin cup of tea. He is perhaps sixteen. He wears no helmet, only a thin smile.

PHILIPPE (CONT'D)
Who are you?

The boy sets the cup down in front of him.

STRANGER
Tea, sir. Compliments of the other side.

Philippe doesn't breathe. The pencil rolls off the table.

STRANGER (CONT'D)
They've been listening since Tuesday.

PHILIPPE
(very quietly)
And you came to tell me?

STRANGER
I came to say they've changed the plan.

He crosses to the radio, turns the dial one click, and Battalion's voice fades to nothing. Another voice takes its place, in another language, bright and brisk.

The boy meets Philippe's eyes. Now he has a name.

ALOIS
My name is Alois, sir. I'd drink the tea.

Philippe picks up the cup. His hand does not shake. He drinks.
